# `integration/js`

`golem-js` publishes `golem-engine`: the browser ESM runtime (`GameClient`, `createChannel`, `PbWriter`/`PbReader`, realtime bootstrap helpers) that generated JS clients import. Source lives in `golem-js/src/`; `dist/` is build output generated by `npm run build`/`npm prepack` and should not be hand-edited or committed.

**Realtime bootstrap** (`bootstrap.ts`): `fetchRealtimeConfig` loads JSON from `golem.Server.RealtimeConfigHandler` (`transport`, `url`, optional hex `serverCertificateHashes`, optional `eventualAckIntervalMs`, and optional WebSocket `fallback`) with injectable `fetch`/`init` and optional `signal` (overrides `init.signal`). Success bodies stream-capped at 64 KiB; error snippets at 512 bytes via `Response.body` cancel-on-limit (safe arrayBuffer/text fallback). Non-2xx/errors must not leak caller query tokens. `eventualAckIntervalMs` absent/zero keeps defaults; reject negatives, fractions, non-finite, and values outside signed int32. `connectOptionsFromRealtimeConfig` / `withQueryParam` / `withQuery` retain primary-only compatibility, while `connectPlanFromRealtimeConfig` builds ordered credential-free candidates with a lazy per-candidate resolver.

**Connection fallback:** `GameClient.connect` accepts legacy strings/options or a `ConnectPlan`. Plans check capability before resolving credentials, allow only WebTransport→WebSocket fallback, dial sequentially, and apply a fixed five-second WebTransport pre-open deadline. Resolver failure aborts the logical attempt; transport setup/handshake/pre-open failure awaits bounded channel closure before advancing to WebSocket with newly resolved options. Post-open closes never switch transport. An opened WebSocket stays preferred for the same credential-free endpoint/hash/ACK plan until direct connect, plan change, or client recreation. Fence all async resolver/channel/timeout callbacks by connection generation and report only the final logical failure. Intentional closure of an established channel emits one clean disconnect; cancelling a pending attempt is silent.

**Credential-safe transport errors:** browser `ErrorEvent.message` and WebSocket/WebTransport runtime `Error` strings can repeat the full connection URL. Built-in channels must not log or forward those raw values; retain the separately redacted URL for diagnostics and normalize runtime failures to credential-free `DisconnectInfo.error` values before application callbacks. Sanitize close reasons and bootstrap error text/body snippets as well as error objects. Invalid URLs are logged only as `<invalid-url>`.

The `js-client` integration emits generated TypeScript in consumer projects from `codegen/templates/js/` plus codec files from `codegen/js_proto.go`. Optional checked protocol constant sets are emitted as `as const` objects plus union types in `entities_pb`. `compileJSIntegration` runs `tsc` in the output dir and removes intermediate `.ts` so consumer output is `.js` plus `.d.ts`.

Transport constants and framing must stay in sync with `golem/net`: reliable frame cap `256 KiB`, WebTransport datagram cap `1200`, datagram header/ACK mask sizes, lane IDs (`1` unreliable, `2` reliable-unordered, `3` reliable-ordered, `4` eventual-state), and control frames `0x00 'OGS' 0x01/0x02`.

`GameClient` exposes two command sends: `send` uses reliable-unordered datagrams on WebTransport, while `sendOrdered` uses reliable-ordered datagrams. Both fall back to the shared reliable-stream `ClientPacket` queue on WebSocket. Keep raw byte sends confined to the low-level channel contracts, enforce each datagram lane's payload budget without stream fallback, and keep generated `send{Name}` / `send{Name}Ordered` helpers aligned.

`GameClient` treats stream `ServerMessage` frames as wrapped protobuf payloads, while compact state datagrams are framed state records and go directly through `EntityManager.applyCompactUpdate`. Keep world updates and server events on the reliable stream path.

Generated managers own client-side state: entity revisions reject stale updates; full state spawns/registers lifecycle hooks; deltas update existing entities only; collection/map deltas use `undefined` as absent and empty values as explicit clears. Entity, world, and event listeners are multicast sets whose registration methods return unsubscribe functions. Entity-targeted events expose typed `(entity, event)` subscriptions while preserving optional entity hooks for advanced model logic. The generated `Client` intersection type exposes each configured entity, world, and event manager as its concrete generated type rather than the optional runtime interface.

When changing JS generated shapes, update `codegen` tests covering emitted TypeScript and runtime tests under `golem-js/test/`; generated protocol changes should stay conceptually parallel with Go proto/runtime paths.
